<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="PowerPC:BE:32:default">
<!--
    kuna option bejoin: a two-register value on a big-endian ABI is joined
    high word first.

    The SVR4 PowerPC ABI returns a long long in r3:r4 with the HIGH word in
    r3, the first register of the pair.  MIPS o32 ($2:$3), SPARC (%o0:%o1) and
    ARM big-endian (r0:r1) do the same: a register pair holds the value as if
    it were loaded from memory, so the lower-addressed, most significant word
    lands in the first register.  The cspec output rule says so (a join rule
    consumes the most significant piece first on a big-endian target), but
    return recovery (ActionReturnRecovery::buildReturnOutput) and the
    call-output pair (FuncCallSpecs::buildOutputFromTrials) joined the first
    register as the LOW word, so every such return printed its halves
    swapped:

        wide_mul:    return CONCAT44(a1 * a0,(int4)(... >> 0x20));   (bug)
        add_one:     return CONCAT44(a1 + 1,a0 + carry);              (bug)
        triple_plus: v1 = (uint4)(v2 >> 0x20) reads r4 as the high word

    Stage: P4 calls and prototypes (output-prototype), option bejoin off|on.
    Pass 1 (off) pins the old first-register-low join, pass 2 (on, the
    shipped default) the ABI's order.

    Source (clang 14 -O2, powerpc-linux-gnu, bytes from bejoin_ppc32_be.o):
        long long wide_mul(int a, int b) { return (long long)a * b; }
          mulhw r5,r4,r3 / mullw r4,r4,r3 / mr r3,r5 / blr
        long long add_one(long long v) { return v + 1; }
          addic r4,r4,1 / addze r3,r3 / blr          (v arrives as r3:r4)
        static long long triple(int x) { return (long long)x * 3; }
        long long triple_plus(int x) { return triple(x) + 7; }
          bl triple / addic r4,r4,7 / addze r3,r3    (a call output pair)

    The trade-off the option makes (clang 14 -O2): a 64-bit sum and the int
    holding its high word are the same instructions, so on reads both as the
    long long and off reads both as the int.
        unsigned long long sum64(u64 a, u64 b) { return a + b; }
        int sum_hi(u64 a, u64 b) { return (a + b) >> 32; }
          addc r4,r6,r4 / adde r3,r5,r3 / blr        (both functions)

    What a comparison leaves behind keeps the old join in both passes (clang
    14 -O0): r3 holds only the carry, r4 the sum it was computed from.
        int carry(u32 a, u32 b) { return (u64)a + b > 0xffffffffULL; }
          li r3,0 / addc r4,r4,r5 / addze r3,r3

    Asserts #1-#3: on joins each value high word (r3) first.
    Asserts #4-#5: off keeps the swapped joins, and only off.
    Asserts #6-#7: sum64 and sum_hi read the same either way.
    Assert #8: carry keeps the old join in both passes.
-->
